# Blocking bridges in PRODUCTION roots (src/, tools/, samples/, clients/).
#
# Seeded 2026-08-30 by ObservableToTaskBridgeGuard.NoNewBlockingBridgeInProductionCode.
# Format: <repo-relative path><TAB><count>.  '#' starts a comment.
#
# 🚨 THIS INVENTORY MAY ONLY SHRINK. Adding a line is not a fix — it is the fix being
# skipped. Fix the site (stay reactive and Subscribe, or go through IIoPool at a genuine
# IO edge), delete the line, and lower ProductionBlockingTotalBudget in the same change.
#
# WHY IT IS SEEDED RATHER THAN EMPTY. Every other rule in ObservableToTaskBridgeGuard holds
# production at ZERO with no list at all, because production genuinely contains no
# unsanctioned observable-to-Task bridge. The BLOCKING shapes do not start from zero: these
# nine sites already existed when the guard was extended, and the sibling guard
# (BlockingBridgeInTestRatchetGuard) had never scanned production roots — it scans test/ only,
# on the stated grounds that src/ is "governed by ... the reviews that enforce it". Reviews
# are not a gate. Holding these at zero on day one would have made the guard red on merge,
# and a rule that reds on arrival gets suppressed rather than obeyed.
#
# The markers are `.Wait()` and `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()`. `.Wait(timeout)` is deliberately
# NOT matched: IoPool's `_gate.Wait(DrainTimeout)` is the SemaphoreSlim AGENTS.md sanctions
# inside IoPool, and HubDisposalJoin's `joined.Wait(budget)` is a documented, bounded
# Task.Wait(TimeSpan). The empty-paren spelling is the UNBOUNDED park, which is the defect.

# The in-mesh sample layout area is GONE from this list. It drained the child's two pipes with
# ReadToEndAsync + GetAwaiter().GetResult(); it now drains them with OutputDataReceived /
# ErrorDataReceived while the pool thread sits in WaitForExit(), which is concurrent, cannot fill
# a pipe, and bridges nothing. The reads were already inside IIoPool.InvokeBlocking, so this was
# never a hub park — it was async-over-sync at a boundary that does not need async at all.

# NodeSetCompiler's Rx .Wait() over the @@-include walk is GONE from this list: the tree bake now
# hands the walk a SYNCHRONOUS reader (NodeCompileShaping.ResolveCodeIncludesInMemory), takes the
# value from the subscription, and throws rather than parks if the chain ever stops completing
# inside Subscribe. src/ carries no blocking bridge.

# Rx's blocking IObservable.Wait() on the Orleans cluster disposal join. Bounded by an
# upstream .Timeout(timeout), whose timer runs on the default scheduler and so always unblocks
# the parked thread — a named failure rather than a wedge.

# Host lifecycle (StartAsync/StopAsync) and a queue drain, blocked from the plugin tester's
# synchronous run boundary.
tools/MeshWeaver.PluginTester/PluginGateRunner.cs	3

# One Rx .Wait() over package discovery and one .GetAwaiter().GetResult() in the bake.
tools/MeshWeaver.PluginTester/TreeBake.cs	2
